      "status": "affected",
      "action_statement": "Update uv to 0.12.17-0 or later.",
      "status_notes": "GHSA-2mjx-qc3c-rqvc (RUSTSEC-2026-0285): rustls 0.23.13 to 0.23.44 accepts TLS 1.3 handshake messages sent at the wrong encryption level when they follow a key-changing message in the same record, so messages that must be encrypted can arrive in plaintext without the connection being rejected. Fixed in rustls 0.23.45: https://github.com/rustls/rustls/security/advisories/GHSA-2mjx-qc3c-rqvc, https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0285.html. Read all 94 uv packages in the DHI Debian repository as of 2026-11-27: every published build, 0.11.22-0 to the current 0.13.10-4, on amd64 and arm64 (47 per architecture), each checked against the checksum in the package index; no debug-symbol packages are listed for this source. Each package holds two dynamically linked ELF programs, /usr/bin/uv and /usr/bin/uvx, plus shell completions, man pages and documentation; no package holds a static archive, PE or Mach-O file. For each program I read the crate list that cargo-auditable embeds and searched the program for the crates' source paths. The list of /usr/bin/uv names one rustls version per build: 0.23.40 to 0.23.43 in 0.11.22-0 to 0.12.15-0, 0.23.45 in 0.12.17-0 to 0.13.4-0, and 0.23.46 in every build from 0.13.4-1 on, and the rustls source paths compiled into /usr/bin/uv name the same version in every build. /usr/bin/uvx is a small launcher that runs uv: its embedded list names the same crates, but in no build does it contain rustls source paths, while the same search finds other crates' paths (gimli, miniz_oxide) in it and finds rustls paths in /usr/bin/uv. Every build before 0.12.17-0 ships an affected rustls and every build from 0.12.17-0 on ships 0.23.45 or later."
    },
